This project began as a fusion/derivative browser skill and retains grateful
attribution to the following upstream work:

1. web-access — https://github.com/eze-is/web-access
   Author: 一泽Eze. License: MIT.
   Historical influences retained in the Rust rewrite include browser
   discovery rules, the local HTTP workflow, and browser-tool routing ideas.

2. chrome-use — https://github.com/leeguooooo/chrome-use
   Author: leeguooooo. License: Apache License 2.0.
   Independently reimplemented ideas include multi-session isolation,
   humanized pointer/typing behavior, network interception, and an extension
   bridge.

3. Tencent BrowserSkill — https://github.com/Tencent/BrowserSkill
   Author: Tencent. Current license: MIT.
   Independently reimplemented product ideas include Agent Windows, semantic
   references, explicit tab borrowing, human handoff, ownership-aware cleanup,
   and per-session operation queues. No Tencent source is vendored here.

Version 2.0 replaces the Node host runtime with original Rust code. The
browser extension remains JavaScript because Chromium MV3 APIs execute in a
JavaScript service worker. Site-specific patterns and executable adapters are
not distributed in this repository; they live in a separately configured
local or Git Knowledge Store.

The optional native saved-tab-group cleaner includes MIT-licensed JavaScript
compiled from chrome-tab-group-cleaner by Franz Enzenhofer:
https://github.com/franzenzenhofer/chrome-tab-group-cleaner
Revision: 3f9d94af3b150583777af27795225e29852a5f80.
See native/vendor/chrome-tab-group-cleaner/LICENSE and README.md.
This optional maintenance component uses Node.js; the browser automation
Runtime continues to use Rust.
